1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,566
KING: You drew a door in my castle. A door that does not exist.

2
00:00:04,946 --> 00:00:07,908
WREN: It's a map, Your Majesty. Maps have mistakes.

3
00:00:08,288 --> 00:00:14,609
KING: Then survive seven trials before the midnight bell. Fail one, and the mistake is you.

4
00:00:14,989 --> 00:00:20,598
This is Wren. She has one day, one candle, and one very large cellmate.

5
00:00:20,978 --> 00:00:23,071
HOB: Hob. Hello. We're going to die.

6
00:00:23,451 --> 00:00:25,890
Survive the King. Day one.

7
00:00:31,470 --> 00:00:37,432
Dawn. Wren's candle will burn for one day. When it's gone, they solve in the dark.

8
00:00:37,812 --> 00:00:41,618
The cell door has a lock with six iron dials.

9
00:00:41,998 --> 00:00:46,961
Five are already set. Two. Six. Twelve. Twenty. Thirty.

10
00:00:47,341 --> 00:00:50,897
WREN: The last dial is blank. Set it right, and the door opens.

11
00:00:51,277 --> 00:00:52,917
HOB: And if we set it wrong?

12
00:00:53,297 --> 00:00:57,967
A spike drops from the ceiling. Hob has seen it happen. Twice.

13
00:00:59,147 --> 00:01:01,862
HOB: I say forty. Round numbers are lucky.

14
00:01:02,242 --> 00:01:03,393
WREN: Don't touch it.

15
00:01:03,773 --> 00:01:08,394
What finishes the lock? Forty, forty-two, or forty-four?

16
00:01:19,074 --> 00:01:23,781
WREN: Look at the gaps. Two to six is four. Six to twelve is six. Then eight. Then ten.

17
00:01:24,161 --> 00:01:27,155
WREN: So the next gap is twelve. Thirty plus twelve.

18
00:01:27,535 --> 00:01:29,102
HOB: I'd have said forty.

19
00:01:29,482 --> 00:01:30,284
WREN: I know.

20
00:01:30,664 --> 00:01:39,051
Forty-two. The lock clicks open. But the corridor outside is pitch black, so Wren lights the only candle they have.

21
00:01:44,431 --> 00:01:53,658
Wren knows these corridors. Three days ago, she sold the King a map of his own castle. In the north wall, she drew a door.

22
00:01:54,038 --> 00:01:55,498
WREN: I drew what I saw.

23
00:01:55,878 --> 00:02:03,989
His guards checked the north wall. Solid stone. So the King wants to know who lied: the map, or the mapmaker.

24
00:02:04,369 --> 00:02:10,084
The corridor ends at an iron gate. One word is carved across it: crown.

25
00:02:10,464 --> 00:02:19,317
Wren reads walls the way she reads maps. Every scratch means something, and the walls here are covered in tally marks.

26
00:02:19,697 --> 00:02:21,588
HOB: Prisoners who reached this gate.

27
00:02:21,968 --> 00:02:23,573
WREN: And how many got through it?

28
00:02:23,953 --> 00:02:25,217
HOB: Let's not count.

29
00:02:25,597 --> 00:02:29,380
KING: Change one letter. Make it what I do to traitors.

30
00:02:29,760 --> 00:02:33,428
HOB: He tells everyone what he does to traitors. He does it in the moat.

31
00:02:33,808 --> 00:02:36,186
Clown, drown, or brown?

32
00:02:46,866 --> 00:02:48,901
WREN: He told us himself. The moat.

33
00:02:49,281 --> 00:02:58,556
Drown. The C becomes a D, and the gate grinds open. But the stairs behind it lead down, toward the sound of water.

34
00:02:58,936 --> 00:03:06,839
Above them, the King listens through a brass speaking tube. Beside him stands his advisor, Master Corvin.

35
00:03:07,219 --> 00:03:10,101
CORVIN: Quick minds make good spies, Your Majesty.

36
00:03:10,481 --> 00:03:13,475
KING: Or good mapmakers. We will see which.

37
00:03:18,855 --> 00:03:22,575
Noon. A long hall, and two guards on patrol.

38
00:03:22,955 --> 00:03:28,577
The tall one passes the door every six minutes. The short one, every eight.

39
00:03:28,957 --> 00:03:34,293
HOB: They both just walked past. When they meet at the door, they stop and gossip. That is our moment.

40
00:03:34,673 --> 00:03:38,961
HOB: I used to walk that patrol. Six minutes. I timed it with my stomach.

41
00:03:39,341 --> 00:03:40,810
WREN: Why did they lock you up?

42
00:03:41,190 --> 00:03:45,798
HOB: I sneezed during a royal speech. It was a big sneeze. His crown fell off.

43
00:03:46,178 --> 00:03:48,557
WREN: So when do they pass the door together again?

44
00:03:48,937 --> 00:03:52,388
Fourteen minutes, twenty-four, or forty-eight?

45
00:04:03,068 --> 00:04:08,958
WREN: Count the tall one. Six, twelve, eighteen, twenty-four. The short one. Eight, sixteen, twenty-four.

46
00:04:09,338 --> 00:04:14,753
WREN: Forty-eight works too, but that is the second time they meet. By then our candle is half gone.

47
00:04:15,133 --> 00:04:21,390
At fourteen minutes, Hob nearly stepped out. The short guard was still standing at the door.

48
00:04:21,770 --> 00:04:28,804
Twenty-four minutes. The guards meet, argue about soup, and two prisoners walk right behind them.

49
00:04:34,184 --> 00:04:42,567
Therefore the next door slams shut behind them. The room is a stone tank, and water pours from a lion's mouth.

50
00:04:42,947 --> 00:04:47,853
KING: The water doubles every minute. In ten minutes, this room is full.

51
00:04:48,233 --> 00:04:53,770
High on the wall is a grate. It sits at exactly half the room's height.

52
00:04:54,150 --> 00:04:56,735
HOB: So we float up to the grate and climb out.

53
00:04:57,115 --> 00:04:59,987
WREN: Only if we're ready the moment the water reaches it.

54
00:05:00,367 --> 00:05:02,081
HOB: Wren. I can't swim.

55
00:05:02,461 --> 00:05:05,413
WREN: You don't have to swim. You have to float, and not panic.

56
00:05:05,793 --> 00:05:07,897
HOB: Panicking is my best skill.

57
00:05:08,277 --> 00:05:10,616
When is the room half full?

58
00:05:10,996 --> 00:05:14,047
Five minutes, nine minutes, or one?

59
00:05:24,727 --> 00:05:27,680
WREN: It doubles. So one minute before it's full, it's half full.

60
00:05:28,060 --> 00:05:35,827
Nine minutes. For eight minutes the water barely covers their boots. Then, in the ninth, it leaps to the grate.

61
00:05:36,207 --> 00:05:43,316
Water to their knees. To their chests. Hob grabs the grate with one hand, and Wren with the other.

62
00:05:43,696 --> 00:05:46,742
They squeeze through. The candle does not.

63
00:05:47,122 --> 00:05:51,602
CORVIN: The candle is gone, Your Majesty. They will never read the chests.

64
00:05:51,982 --> 00:05:54,817
KING: Then we'll learn what she is willing to burn.

65
00:05:55,197 --> 00:06:00,020
Now they're in the dark. So the next trial is going to cost something.

66
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:11,557
Dusk. They hear the next trial before they see it. Coins, clinking in three chests.

67
00:06:11,937 --> 00:06:22,958
KING: Three chests. One holds gold, one holds lead, one holds both. Every label is wrong. Take one coin from one chest, then name all three.

68
00:06:23,338 --> 00:06:25,395
HOB: We can't read labels in the dark.

69
00:06:25,775 --> 00:06:31,720
Wren has one thing that burns. The map. The only proof that her door is real.

70
00:06:33,600 --> 00:06:37,605
WREN: My father taught me to draw. This is the best map I ever made.

71
00:06:37,985 --> 00:06:39,923
HOB: Then it'll make the best light.

72
00:06:40,303 --> 00:06:44,410
She lights it. The labels read: gold. Lead. Mixed.

73
00:06:44,790 --> 00:06:48,887
Which chest should she draw from? Gold, lead, or mixed?

74
00:06:59,567 --> 00:07:03,792
HOB: Take one from the chest marked gold. If it is gold, we are rich.

75
00:07:04,172 --> 00:07:08,296
WREN: A gold coin from there could mean all gold or mixed. It tells us nothing.

76
00:07:08,676 --> 00:07:12,917
WREN: The one marked mixed. Its label is wrong, so it's all gold or all lead.

77
00:07:13,297 --> 00:07:21,277
WREN: Say the coin is gold. The chest marked lead can't be lead, and gold is taken, so it's mixed. The chest marked gold holds the lead.

78
00:07:21,657 --> 00:07:26,774
Three chests, three right names. The door opens. The map is ash.

79
00:07:27,154 --> 00:07:29,724
WREN: It doesn't matter. I remember every line.

80
00:07:30,104 --> 00:07:32,465
HOB: Good. Because I don't remember anything.

81
00:07:37,845 --> 00:07:44,581
Night. A door with letters burned into it. Above them, a key: L, J, O, H means king.

82
00:07:44,961 --> 00:07:50,082
WREN: Every letter is one step ahead in the alphabet. L is really K. J is really I.

83
00:07:50,462 --> 00:07:54,983
The password is T, Q, Z. Spy, sky, or toy?

84
00:07:55,363 --> 00:07:58,865
HOB: Sky. It has to be sky. Everyone loves the sky.

85
00:08:09,545 --> 00:08:15,599
Step each letter back. T becomes S. Q becomes P. Z becomes Y.

86
00:08:15,979 --> 00:08:18,719
WREN: Sky would have been T, L, Z. There is no L.

87
00:08:19,099 --> 00:08:19,785
WREN: Spy.

88
00:08:20,165 --> 00:08:22,358
HOB: Why would the password be spy?

89
00:08:22,738 --> 00:08:32,037
KING: Because three nights ago, a spy walked into my castle through a door that does not exist. And the only map that shows it was yours.

90
00:08:32,417 --> 00:08:35,756
WREN: I didn't let anyone in. I only drew what was already there.

91
00:08:36,136 --> 00:08:39,570
KING: Then you will show me where it is. Before midnight.

92
00:08:39,950 --> 00:08:43,941
Behind the King, Master Corvin's quill stops moving.

93
00:08:44,321 --> 00:08:48,759
CORVIN: Your Majesty, if she were the spy, why would she draw her own door?

94
00:08:49,139 --> 00:08:52,363
KING: That is exactly what I intend to find out.

95
00:08:52,743 --> 00:08:58,555
So the trials were never a punishment. They were a test of who Wren really is.

96
00:09:03,935 --> 00:09:08,270
Midnight. The last door opens only while the bell is ringing.

97
00:09:08,650 --> 00:09:17,466
KING: At six, my bell rings six times. From the first ring to the last takes five seconds. At midnight, it rings twelve.

98
00:09:17,846 --> 00:09:23,014
From the first ring to the last: ten seconds, eleven, or twelve?

99
00:09:33,694 --> 00:09:38,531
WREN: Six rings have five gaps. One second each. Twelve rings have eleven gaps.

100
00:09:38,911 --> 00:09:43,662
Eleven seconds. They run on the first ring. But Hob is slow.

101
00:09:45,042 --> 00:09:46,291
HOB: Go without me!

102
00:09:46,671 --> 00:09:47,842
WREN: Not a chance.

103
00:09:48,222 --> 00:09:51,647
On the eleventh second, Wren drags him through.

104
00:09:55,427 --> 00:10:03,512
They stumble into the King's own bedchamber. Behind them, in the north wall, is a door. The door Wren drew.

105
00:10:03,892 --> 00:10:07,967
KING: A door that does not exist. And yet, here you stand.

106
00:10:08,347 --> 00:10:12,638
In the corner, Master Corvin quietly pushes the door shut.

107
00:10:13,018 --> 00:10:19,795
KING: Day two begins at dawn. One of you walks free. The other hangs for the spy. You choose.

108
00:10:20,175 --> 00:10:22,040
Day two: the choice.

